LG:
  - Lightweight GL
  - Leyun's GL

Memo on caveats:

---

## Complexity Estimate

### Project Overview

LG is a modular OpenGL 3.x framework written in C++17 (~2,676 lines across 48 files).
It provides a rendering engine, shader/buffer abstractions, geometric shapes, fractal
generators, and interactive example applications.

### Component Breakdown

| Component                        | Files                                                       | LOC  | Complexity |
|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|------|------------|
| Core rendering engine            | GLApplication, GL3DApplication, GLProgram, GLShader,        | ~400 | High       |
|                                  | GLBuffer, GLVertexArray, GLImage                            |      |            |
| Geometry abstractions            | GLGeometry, GLBasicGeometry, GLCompositeGeometry,           | ~200 | Medium     |
|                                  | GLTransformedGeometry                                       |      |            |
| Basic shapes                     | GLTriangle, GLCircle, GLSphere, GLAxes, GLSmell             | ~260 | Low–Medium |
| Fractals & procedural generation | SierpinskiGasket, SierpinskiGasket2, KochSnowflake,         | ~550 | Medium–High|
|                                  | BarnsleyFern, Maze                                          |      |            |
| Example applications             | GLApplicationExample, GLTriangleExample, GLSphereExample,   | ~600 | Medium     |
|                                  | SierpinskiGasketExample, MazeExample                        |      |            |
| Utilities & build system         | Utils, Global, CMakeLists.txt                               | ~130 | Low        |

Key design patterns used: Abstract Factory, Composite, Decorator, Singleton,
Template Method, and RAII with std::shared_ptr throughout.

### Notable Complexity Points

- Maze (393 LOC): the single most complex component. Implements three independent
  maze-generation algorithms (depth-first search backtracking, Prim's algorithm,
  Kruskal's union-find), entry/exit placement, cell graph representation, and
  geometry emission for the renderer.
- GLProgram (181 LOC): manages shader linking, cached uniform locations, and
  overloaded vertex-attribute setters for scalar, vec2/3/4, and mat3/4 types.
- GLApplication (168 LOC): singleton GLUT callback routing, projection/view/model
  matrix stack, blend/depth state, and screen-capture support.
- Fractal generators: SierpinskiGasket grows as O(3^order) triangles, KochSnowflake
  as O(4^order) segments; BarnsleyFern and the iterative Sierpinski variant handle
  up to ~1 million points with IFS / weighted-random-transform selection.

### Estimated Development Time

These estimates assume a developer already familiar with C++17 and the OpenGL API.

| Component                        | Estimated time    |
|----------------------------------|-------------------|
| Core rendering engine            | 2–3 days          |
| Geometry abstractions            | 1 day             |
| Basic shapes                     | 1–2 days          |
| Fractals (Sierpinski, Koch, Fern)| 1–2 days          |
| Maze generator (3 algorithms)    | 2–3 days          |
| Example applications             | 1–2 days          |
| Build system & utilities         | 0.5 day           |
| **Total (experienced developer)**| **~9–14 days**    |

For a developer learning OpenGL or the underlying mathematics concurrently,
add a factor of 2–3x, giving a realistic range of **3–6 weeks**.
